WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.460
From guest appearances as street magicians to&nbsp;
recurring roles as psychotic plastic surgeons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.205
Castle has hosted a lot of&nbsp;
famous faces in its cast.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.320
Annie Wersching played the role of Dr. Kelly&nbsp;
Nieman in Seasons 6 and 7 on Castle. Neiman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.640
was a villainess and plastic surgeon who secretly&nbsp;
worked alongside Michael Mosley's serial killer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.700
Jerry Tyson. Wersching's performance&nbsp;
stood out as one of the strongest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.460
one-off villains in the series for&nbsp;
her calculating, demented doctor.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.600
"Good. You're awake. We can get started."

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.520
Wersching initially gained fame for her role as&nbsp;
Renee Walker in Seasons 7 and 8 of the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.740
24 — an extremely tough, willful, and complex&nbsp;
character frequently butting heads with Jack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.120
Bauer. Other significant roles in her filmography&nbsp;
include Amelia Joffe in General Hospital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.620
Julia Brasher in Bosch, Emma Whitmore in&nbsp;
Timeless, and the Borg Queen in Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.840
Picard. She also gained critical acclaim for her&nbsp;
motion-capture performance as Tess in the 2013&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:01.114
video game, The Last of Us. Wersching tragically&nbsp;
passed away from cancer on January 29, 2023.

00:01:01.946 --> 00:01:06.420
You may not have realized Chadwick Boseman was&nbsp;
even in Castle, but he appears in Episode 12 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.380
Season 3 as a magician named Chuck Russell.&nbsp;
Although he's only there as the red herring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.340
suspect for the murder of a fellow magician,&nbsp;
Boseman stands out in his rather silly role.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.720
Boseman is adored worldwide for his starring&nbsp;
role as T'Challa the Black Panther in the Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.220
Cinematic Universe. He made his first appearance&nbsp;
as the character in 2016's Captain America:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:28.200
Civil War but was given his own film in 2018.&nbsp;
He continued the role throughout Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.620
Infinity War and Endgame until&nbsp;
his tragic passing in 2020 from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:36.300
colon cancer. He was praised for his acting&nbsp;
talent, charitable work, and philanthropy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.140
with many celebrating his legacy in&nbsp;
popular culture, including a statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:44.460
by Black Panther director Ryan Coogler.&nbsp;
According to Den of Geek, Coogler claimed,

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.540
"[Boseman] was such an integral part of that&nbsp;
character for us, both as the character and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.970
as a person, that we could not conceive of&nbsp;
a version with having someone else on set."

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.600
Although Len Lesser plays a very small role&nbsp;
in Castle, the famous character actor is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.440
still remembered for his part in the second&nbsp;
episode of Season 1. Lesser makes his minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.680
appearance count as the funny distraction&nbsp;
from an ongoing murder investigation.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.820
"He's not a cop. I'm a cop."
"And you're looking for someone to have sex with?"

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.560
Lesser may be familiar to audiences from&nbsp;
his standout role as Uncle Leo in the hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:18.840
NBC sitcom Seinfeld. His character made his first&nbsp;
appearance in the 2nd season of the show as Jerry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.040
Seinfeld's eccentric uncle who frequently gives&nbsp;
his nephew trouble about a variety of overblown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.900
inconveniences. Uncle Leo was so beloved,&nbsp;
he's ranked at #6 on Rolling Stone's "100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.960
Best Seinfeld Characters" list. Sadly, Len Lesser&nbsp;
passed away in 2011 at the age of 88. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:38.040
to HuffPost, Jerry Seinfeld made an official&nbsp;
statement following Lesser's death, stating,

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:43.260
"Len was one of our favorites. We always loved&nbsp;
having him on the show. I'll never forget when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.700
Uncle Leo was in prison and tattooed 'Jerry&nbsp;
Hello' on his knuckles. He was a very sweet guy."

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:53.160
Chad Everett was a prolific actor who appeared in&nbsp;
almost 100 movies and television shows over his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:58.200
50-year career. His last credited on-screen&nbsp;
role was in Season 4, Episode 14 of Castle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.000
where he plays Jerry Maddox, a former private&nbsp;
investigator going under the name Joe Flynn.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.140
While this role can be considered a footnote&nbsp;
in his career, the appearance came just before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.100
his death from lung cancer in July of&nbsp;
2012. Everett was mainly known for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:16.380
portrayal of Dr. Joe Gannon in the CBS&nbsp;
series Medical Center from 1969 to 1976,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:21.110
with his other notable parts being in Airplane&nbsp;
II: The Sequel, The Nanny, and Mulholland Drive.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.280
Unlike any of the other Castle actors on this&nbsp;
list, Wes Craven was the only one to make a cameo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.840
appearance as himself during Season 5. The episode&nbsp;
revolved around Castle and Beckett investigating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.100
the death of a woman who was convinced she was&nbsp;
cursed from watching a haunted video tape. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:39.660
episode's obvious homage to horror flicks like&nbsp;
The Ring explains why horror icon Craven shows up.

00:03:39.867 --> 00:03:42.327
"Is Stephen King beating you&nbsp;
at Texas Hold 'Em again?"&nbsp;

00:03:42.358 --> 00:03:43.743
"Yeah, right."

00:03:43.743 --> 00:03:48.180
Craven is best known for the cult classics The&nbsp;
Last House on the Left and The Hills Have Eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.560
as well as the massive horror franchises&nbsp;
A Nightmare on Elm Street and Scream.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.640
Craven passed away from a brain&nbsp;
tumor in 2015 at the age of 76,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.320
and is remembered as a pioneer of horror&nbsp;
moviemaking. During one interview with fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.560
horror director Mick Garris, Craven&nbsp;
summed up his filmmaking philosophy,

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.500
"You realize you're doing something&nbsp;
that means something to people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.243
so — Shut up and get back to work."

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:15.420
Fans of Castle will no doubt remember Nathaniel&nbsp;
Marston's performance as Grant Vyro in the Season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:20.040
3 episode titled "The Last Call." Vyro was a&nbsp;
union dock worker operating under the false&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:25.140
identity of a dead man, but — ultimately — had a&nbsp;
verifiable alibi to clear him of any wrongdoing.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:30.180
Marston was a successful actor who was mainly&nbsp;
known for his roles on the ABC soap opera One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:35.520
Life to Live. Marston played the character Al&nbsp;
Holden from 2001 to 2003, and became Dr. Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:41.700
McBain from 2004 to 2007, after Holden dies and&nbsp;
his spirit transitions into McBain. Marston had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:46.980
other minor roles in Law &amp; Order: Special Victims&nbsp;
Unit, White Collar, and Blue Bloods. Marston died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:51.575
from surgery complications in 2015, after&nbsp;
he was severely injured in a car accident.

00:04:51.834 --> 00:04:55.020
Robin Sachs appeared as the&nbsp;
Announcer in Season 4, Episode 13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.960
in which Castle investigates&nbsp;
the death of a dog trainer.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:04.790
"Only one question remains. Who&nbsp;
will be this year's Best in Show?"

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:09.360
Sachs became well known for his performances&nbsp;
in film, television, and theater over his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.560
decades-long career. He is probably best&nbsp;
known for his part as Ethan Rayne in Buffy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:17.820
the Vampire Slayer, a recurring antagonist&nbsp;
against Buffy and her crew. Sachs was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:23.100
known for many minor roles, including The Lost&nbsp;
World: Jurassic Park, Galaxy Quest, and Babylon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.980
5. Sachs passed away from a fatal heart attack in&nbsp;
2013, just four days short of his 62nd birthday.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:33.420
Although only supplying a small cameo as&nbsp;
one of Castle's regular poker buddies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:37.440
the story of Stephen J. Cannell goes much&nbsp;
deeper than that. Cannell was a writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.520
producer, and novelist who had a hand&nbsp;
in many successful television series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.480
which includes The A-Team,&nbsp;
Renegade, and 21 Jump Street.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:49.020
Cannell openly struggled with dyslexia&nbsp;
throughout his early life and career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:52.620
some of which was chronicled in&nbsp;
the 2012 documentary Dislecksia:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:56.880
The Movie. He was an important factor&nbsp;
in the creation of Castle and — as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.720
result — he was given a special tribute at&nbsp;
the end of a Season 3 episode which read,

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.880
"Stephen J. Cannell. Colleague.&nbsp;
Mentor. Friend. We'll miss you, pal."

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:11.390
Cannell passed away in September of 2010 due to&nbsp;
complications from melanoma at the age of 69.

00:06:11.734 --> 00:06:14.314
When it comes to hard-working&nbsp;
character actors in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.314 --> 00:06:19.020
few were as impressive as Jon Polito. While&nbsp;
his only minor role in Castle was as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:23.520
witness convinced that the world is still in the&nbsp;
1970s, he nonetheless stood out as a highlight.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.460
"That's a nice dress. Can I talk you out of it?"

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:33.420
Polito was a unique character actor who started&nbsp;
in TV and movies in the 1980s with roles in The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:38.100
Gangster Chronicles, Highlander, and the Coen&nbsp;
brothers' Miller's Crossing — arguably stealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:42.840
the show as the weaselly Johnny Caspar. Polito's&nbsp;
profile grew as he snagged bigger roles in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:47.640
Coen brothers' films Barton Fink and The Big&nbsp;
Lebowski, as well as appearing as Bigelow in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:53.640
The Rocketeer, and Gideon the pawn shop owner in&nbsp;
The Crow. He passed away in 2016 at the age of 65.

